'' STATIC locals. Measured from fbc (docs/internals/phase-6-shared.md):
''
''     void P( void ) { static int32 N$1 = 5;  N$1 = N$1 + 1; }
''
'' A frame slot would be reinitialised on every call, which is the entire behaviour the
'' keyword exists to provide.
'' TEST: cli
'' ARGS: --dump-ir tests/mir/src/static.bas
'' EXPECT-EXIT: 0
'' EXPECT-OUT: verify: ok
'' EXPECT-OUT: 0 scope imbalance

'' Module storage with a compile-time initialiser, not a store executed on entry.
'' EXPECT-OUT: global var i32 P$n = 5
'' EXPECT-OUT: global var agg P$s size 24

'' THE POINT OF THIS TEST. fbc names P's and Q's counters both N$1 and relies on C
'' function scoping; MIR has one flat global table, so without the procedure in the name
'' these two silently become ONE variable and P and Q share a counter.
'' EXPECT-OUT: global var i32 Q$n
'' EXPECT-OUT: store @@P$n
'' EXPECT-OUT: store @@Q$n

'' No frame slot, and no cleanup for the static string -- releasing module storage at a
'' scope end would free it while later calls still use it.
'' EXPECT-NOT: alloca ptr n
'' EXPECT-NOT: fb_StrDelete(@@P$s
'' EXPECT-NOT: <NO TERMINATOR>
'' GOLDEN: expected/static.out

'' Storage is keyed by SYMBOL, not by spelling: two declarations are two symbols even
'' when they share a name, so these must be two variables.
'' EXPECT-OUT: global var i32 SIBLINGS$k
'' EXPECT-OUT: global var i32 SIBLINGS$k$2
'' EXPECT-OUT: store @@SIBLINGS$k,
'' EXPECT-OUT: store @@SIBLINGS$k$2,
